Let  $S$  be the set  $\{1, \dots, n\}$ . For any subset  $I \subset S$ , we write  $E_I$  for the event  $\bigcap_{i \in S} E_i \bigcap_{j \not\in S} E_j^c$ .

The hypothesis on  $a_i$  says nothing but the following: for any  $I \subseteq S$  with  $E_I$  nonempty, and for any  $i \in I$ , we have  $a_i \ge \#I$ .

Thus we have
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i \in S} \frac {P(E_i)}{a_i} &= \sum_{i \in S}\frac 1{a_i}\sum_{I \subseteq S, i \in I}P(E_I)
&= \sum_{I \subseteq S, E_I \neq \emptyset} P(E_I) \sum_{i \in I} \frac 1{a_i}
&\le \sum_{I \subseteq S, E_I \neq \emptyset} P(E_I) \sum_{i \in I} \frac 1{\#I}
&= \sum_{I \subseteq S} P(E_I)
&= 1.
\end{align*}

