Consider  $x_i$  as vectors. Let  $S=\{1,2,3, \ldots, 2m\}$ , and choose randomly a subset  $A$  of cardinality  $m$  of  $S$ . Let  $\varepsilon_{i}\in\{-1,1\}$  be a random variable wich indicates whether  $i$  is in the chosen set or not.

It is obvious that  $\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_{i}^2]=1$ , and for  $i\neq j$  we have 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_{i}\varepsilon{j}] & = \mathbb{P}[i\in A, j \in A]+\mathbb{P}[i\not \in A, j\not \in A]-\mathbb{P}[i\in A, j\not\in A] -\mathbb{P}[i\not \in A, j\in A] 
&=2\frac{\binom{2m-2}{m-2}}{\binom{2m}{m}}-2\frac{\binom{2m-2}{m-1}}{\binom{2m}{m}}
&=-\frac{1}{2m-1}
\end{align*}

The quantity  $\frac{|\sum_{i=1}^{2m} \varepsilon_{i} x_i|^2}{m^2}$  is the square of the distance between the centers of gravity of the  $m$  points with indices in  $A$  and the remaining points.

Now, we have
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{(\sum_{i=1}^{2m} \varepsilon_{i}x_i)^2}{m^2}\right] &=\frac{\mathbb{E}[\sum_{i=1}^{2m} \varepsilon_{i}^2 x_i^2+2\sum_{i<j}\varepsilon_{i}\varepsilon_{j} x_ix_j]}{m^2} 
&= \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{2m} \mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_{i}^2]+2\sum_{i<j}x_ix_j\mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_i \varepsilon{j}]}{m^2}
&=\frac{2m-2 \frac{1}{2m-1} (\sum_{i=1}^{2m}x_i)^{2}-2m)}{m^2}
&\leq \frac{2m(1+2/(2m-1))}{m^2}
&\leq \frac{4}{m}
\end{align*}
So the expectation of the square of the distance is at most  $\frac{4}{m}$  so the expectation of the distance is at most  $\frac{2}{\sqrt{m}}$  therefore we can choose such  $m$  points.

